[{:db/id 17592186152199 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9b739dd6-c1ea-5425-a4f3-ff629b5d83cc"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152006 :question/problem "![](images/73/7325a35720157e2331ade1b9abd042f90030361c1560dd3bf98de08cc495e4c4.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n![](images/bf/bf3c5a6d11a579553b45d0b0c0075a527953e4ffe825427d53839dbcd8e16e7d.svg)\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nI. $R=[-2,2] \\times [-2,1]$\n\nII. $R=[-2,1] \\times [-2,2]$\n\nIII. $R=[-1,1] \\times [-1,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n![](images/3d/3dfc3848c566e7648a776c35b9de046a2d90904d5a6d1779643d696ebbe4f307.svg)\n\nthen, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D (x^2-y^2) \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and II cover the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Rectangle III does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152007 :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve $x^2+y^2=9.$ If \n![](images/e8/e83d9c7f3dd0397ea9a748ef4d6ceb15ff14b0f72166767924a93ef41b796a5a.svg) \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{\\large R} \\cos(x-y) \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_{\\large D} f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A,\\quad$ where $R=[-4,4] \\times [-4,4]$\n\nII. $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{\\large D} \\cos(x-y) \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_{\\large R} f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A,\\quad$ where $R=[-2,2] \\times [-2,2]$\n\nIII. $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{\\large D} \\cos(x-y) \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_{\\large R} f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A,\\quad$ where $R=[-3,3] \\times [-3,3]$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/3a/3aab7a115fe72d3796be28c66f4ce9ae2c15dc56e09efd98e5ad9a45edebd704.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n![](images/c5/c598ed6ff9209d8456e36df1b0a140ddb43150a5c3c7060232603be0ae4598f3.svg)\n\nthen, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{\\large D} \\cos(x-y) \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_{\\large R} f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement II is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152198 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-fac0df1b-f9cc-511c-8006-433930d0444f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152005 :question/problem "![](images/11/1191de029d927394dcc587f9c432a2d7cf4cec136e6d6ebbe62799de7b4f9b3e.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-1,1] \\times [0,1],$ and\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D |x-y| \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{aligned}|x-y|, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ |x-y|, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}|x-y|, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-9b739dd6-c1ea-5425-a4f3-ff629b5d83cc" :question/id #uuid "9b739dd6-c1ea-5425-a4f3-ff629b5d83cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-129653" :question/problem "![](images/55/555eec8e88dd658903e19b8779f85598f57126b054d5058b662824f09de0f6cb.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-x, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-x)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nI. $R=[-1,1]\\times [-1,1]$\n\nII. $R=[-1,1]\\times [-1,2]$\n\nIII. $R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,0]$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-x, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-x)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and III do not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangle II covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3c6b02fe-a198-551b-8207-64840a262a3d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3c6b02fe-a198-551b-8207-64840a262a3d" :answer-field/id #uuid "3c6b02fe-a198-551b-8207-64840a262a3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-264cf4e2-8ed4-58f5-90ff-ad6c657b4128" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b82083db-b8de-5a77-af8d-e0d8604e5a00" "answer-dcd8a44b-f98b-5eb8-bbd1-c98d25b54697" "answer-74756762-e410-57d4-95d6-39a30bfc37b5" "answer-264cf4e2-8ed4-58f5-90ff-ad6c657b4128" "answer-a6c2f725-8315-5690-b52c-5a28778570c6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b82083db-b8de-5a77-af8d-e0d8604e5a00" :answer/id #uuid "b82083db-b8de-5a77-af8d-e0d8604e5a00" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dcd8a44b-f98b-5eb8-bbd1-c98d25b54697" :answer/id #uuid "dcd8a44b-f98b-5eb8-bbd1-c98d25b54697" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-74756762-e410-57d4-95d6-39a30bfc37b5" :answer/id #uuid "74756762-e410-57d4-95d6-39a30bfc37b5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-264cf4e2-8ed4-58f5-90ff-ad6c657b4128" :answer/id #uuid "264cf4e2-8ed4-58f5-90ff-ad6c657b4128" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6c2f725-8315-5690-b52c-5a28778570c6" :answer/id #uuid "a6c2f725-8315-5690-b52c-5a28778570c6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186354529 :question/problem "![](images/53/534f6ef0206bd4281320968c8a60b88f7f237e48c31e3d10c1080ddb69b9f56f.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{3}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nI. $R=[-1,2]\\times [0,1]$\n\nII. $R=[-1,2]\\times [-2,2]$\n\nIII. $R=[-2,2]\\times [0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{3}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and III do not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangle II covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152824 :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve ${x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}xy, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nI. $R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,1]$\n\nII. $R=[-1,1]\\times [-2,2]$\n\nIII. $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/73/736575dceb6969f8b45e4038f702ebc84347ab66a1a8e5ea5103840ddd61ba31.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}xy, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangle I does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangles II and III cover the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186265765 :question/problem "![](images/b9/b946843e712c6294e261cb3015b02bc065cd265763805cf046009a2db0d11b83.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nI. $R=[-1,3]\\times [-2,1]$\n\nII. $R=[-1,2]\\times [-2,2]$\n\nIII. $R=[-2,3]\\times [-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and II do not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangle III covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186354530 :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x-y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${∬}_{D}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$\n\nII. ${∬}_{R}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$\n\nIII. ${∬}_{D}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/62/62925844de27c9731b181f17a1bdd912bfa3265176bd62ebda425d182ae8d9e8.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x-y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The rectangle $R$ does not completely cover the region $D.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$ Also, the integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186382400 :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${∬}_{R}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-2,2]$\n\nII. ${∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$\n\nIII. ${∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-3,3]$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/5c/5c5dbc673c2c6a9bd0a4f06223133e21e2dcbbdbaa2dfe2de2b29d8824dd821e.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$ Also, the integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statements II and III are true. In both cases, the rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186407297 :question/problem "![](images/51/51706be38cc1c72079fe702c899b575ea53ba4d85e7990a6aa81f20dc8da4de3.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true? \n\nI. ${∬}_{D}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,2]\\times [0,3]$\n\nII. ${∬}_{R}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-5,5]\\times [0,3]$\n\nIII. ${∬}_{D}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[0,2]\\times [-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement II is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement III is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-fac0df1b-f9cc-511c-8006-433930d0444f" :question/id #uuid "fac0df1b-f9cc-511c-8006-433930d0444f" :question/math-academy-id "q-80568" :question/problem "![](images/12/12acb33aa174f1c174a538a89881dd19245f733c6b39df37f9ebbdc1f86442d5.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-3,1]\\times [-1,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({2}^{x}-y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x}-y, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x}-y, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x}-y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/56/56f6557f98674b0f651a781e6226ba9d207273e30136e3396c3aabcf91a64024.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal ${2}^{x}-y$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({2}^{x}-y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use functions I and II. Both of these functions are not identically zero for all points in $R∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(1,0)\\in R∖D$, but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Function III equals ${2}^{x}-y$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ebe09a19-f78b-5d26-9864-3cc9c0f88600"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ebe09a19-f78b-5d26-9864-3cc9c0f88600" :answer-field/id #uuid "ebe09a19-f78b-5d26-9864-3cc9c0f88600" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8b2af876-19a0-51e0-afde-f9a9bf4046f3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-02227aca-c031-59fd-93c4-2fafbc07e7f9" "answer-8df20bc4-f189-5c1c-a2c5-e4c7ac3802cd" "answer-78cab8ed-0dae-5d25-9514-1cf1de27f303" "answer-6f3c18f9-58fa-5eac-9421-703a0fbb6877" "answer-8b2af876-19a0-51e0-afde-f9a9bf4046f3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-02227aca-c031-59fd-93c4-2fafbc07e7f9" :answer/id #uuid "02227aca-c031-59fd-93c4-2fafbc07e7f9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8df20bc4-f189-5c1c-a2c5-e4c7ac3802cd" :answer/id #uuid "8df20bc4-f189-5c1c-a2c5-e4c7ac3802cd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-78cab8ed-0dae-5d25-9514-1cf1de27f303" :answer/id #uuid "78cab8ed-0dae-5d25-9514-1cf1de27f303" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6f3c18f9-58fa-5eac-9421-703a0fbb6877" :answer/id #uuid "6f3c18f9-58fa-5eac-9421-703a0fbb6877" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b2af876-19a0-51e0-afde-f9a9bf4046f3" :answer/id #uuid "8b2af876-19a0-51e0-afde-f9a9bf4046f3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186265783 :question/problem "![](images/f8/f8cb975b622bfe420d9418587293e83de60c297019f15e1b379bc118e079d35d.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-1,2]\\times [-2,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186380838 :question/problem "![](images/e2/e2ded513c72c8029b7d2909d2e3b37cbd659c4ba60a7be72c81379626a7a1234.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-1,2]\\times [-1,3],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186380845 :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1.$ If $R=[-3,3]\\times [-2,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}y{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ y{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186153391 :tutorial/content "We know how to define the double integral of a function over a rectangular domain. Now, we'll learn how to define a double integral over a non-rectangular region.\n\nLet's consider the non-rectangular region $D$ enclosed by the circle $x^2+y^2=1$ in the first quadrant, as shown below. \n\n![](images/e8/e80bcbaff16282f00e7e3df487d78cfbc51bb2e9660dde5e2fc421cd4d70a9af.png)\n\n \n\nWe will define the following double integral over this non-rectangular region:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} (3-x^2-2y^2) \\, \\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nTo do this, we will use the following trick. Let's enclose the region $D$ by a rectangle $R$ with sides parallel to the coordinates axes, as shown below. \n\n![](images/21/214db95d949a8658e3fdbfea8f54c0e3bf914c11d9ef88e1ca4264d64d360c90.png)\n\n \n\nNow, we extend our function to $R$ by setting a new function $f(x,y)$ that's equal to $0$ outside $D$ and equals $3-x^2-2y^2$ on $D\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/ab/ab8eed93d71415d810f4c8c9b19d690317769e270805a6925d35f5f0e4c9671f.svg)\n\n![](images/43/43288ccc496832a1a78f9fcaaabb4a5841fc79db5d2082dff7d4177005b61d01.png)\n\n \n\nAlthough $f(x,y)$ might not be continuous at the boundary of $D,$ the double integral of $f(x,y)$ over the rectangular domain $R$ will exist. \n\nTherefore, we define our double integral of $3-x^2-2y^2$ over $D$ as \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D (3-x^2-2y^2) \\, \\textrm{d}A = \\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nGeometrically, the value of the double integral will give us the volume of the solid bounded by the surface $z=3-x^2-2y^2$ over the quarter-circular domain $D.$\n\n**Watch out!** The domain $R$ must not necessarily be the smallest possible rectangle that covers the region $D.$ It can be any rectangular domain that covers $D$ completely." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152328 :activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
